From f25c1b52b7fb831c3bdbaab87a279dcafa9963fa Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: shiro <build@shiro>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 03:10:06 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Shiro: the wasm JIT compiles code in writable pages, and
 drops it when the bytes it came from are written

The JIT compiled only from pages that aren't writable, so everything a
runtime generates into RWX memory (HotSpot's code cache, V8's, JavaScriptCore's)
ran in the interpreter: 345 M of the main thread's instructions in a cold
javac, 941 M of 1.35 G in Debian's node running a loop.

A writable page code is compiled from gets PAGE_WJC in its page table entry.
No write translation is cached for such a page (WjFill refuses it, the other
threads drop their caches when the mark is set), so every store to it comes
through LookupAddressFor: a store in compiled code leaves its region and the
interpreter runs it. LookupAddressFor calls WjWrote, with the length where
the caller knows it. Each region records the 64-byte pieces of each marked
page it was decoded from (its instructions and what the flag crawler read);
a write that touches one is logged, every thread drops the regions that
depend on it before it next enters compiled code, and the writer leaves the
regions it is in. A write beside the code costs only the slow path; a page
that takes too many of them in a second is given back to the interpreter
for a while. wjit.c, "code on writable pages", has the rules.

What it costs code that never generates any: nothing on a store (the test
shares the copy-on-write test's branch in LookupAddressFor; WjFill tests one
more bit on a miss) and nothing on a region entry (the same epoch compare).

Also:
- callers that only read say so (Load, pops, ReserveAddress for reading,
  CopyFromUser, SchlepR, LoadStr, a read's iovecs, the source of movs, the
  aligned SSE operand as a source), so reading a constant beside code
  doesn't drop it;
- MADV_DONTNEED of a marked page reports the write;
- the flag crawler reads only pages code may be compiled from, and what it
  reads in a marked page is part of the region's dependencies (it used to
  read writable pages with nothing to notice a later change);
- WjExecute's chain stops when a region left for the interpreter to run the
  instruction at m->ip (it entered the same block up to 64 times);
- a compile that can't take a page now (given up lately, locked by a system
  call, a thread in the middle of an instruction without the GIL) is tried
  again after another BLINK_WJIT_THRESHOLD executions, not marked failed.

BLINK_WJIT_RWX=0 leaves writable pages to the interpreter, as before. 3 and
4 are for the tests (fixtures/x86/smcjit.c): 3 ignores the writes, 4 ignores
mprotect and munmap too. SHIRO_BLINK_PROFILE and BLINK_WJIT_DEBUG print the
counts (pages marked, writes that hit code or fell beside it, regions
dropped, pages given up).
